1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,702
Two lectures ago, the honest caveat said a five-step agent makes five separate

2
00:00:04,702 --> 00:00:06,320
chances to be wrong, not one.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:11,007
Here's exactly what that costs, in a number you can compute yourself in ten seconds.

4
00:00:11,007 --> 00:00:15,415
A demo runs an agent through the same two or three steps a dozen times until it

5
00:00:15,415 --> 00:00:16,252
looks polished.

6
00:00:16,252 --> 00:00:20,716
Production runs it thousands of times, often chaining five, eight, ten steps per

7
00:00:20,716 --> 00:00:20,995
task.

8
00:00:20,995 --> 00:00:25,124
Same per-step accuracy, wildly different odds of the whole chain finishing

9
00:00:25,124 --> 00:00:25,682
correctly.

10
00:00:25,682 --> 00:00:27,803
That gap is not a bug in your rollout.

11
00:00:27,803 --> 00:00:28,695
It's arithmetic.

12
00:00:28,695 --> 00:00:32,043
You don't need a statistics background to follow it, either.

13
00:00:32,043 --> 00:00:36,730
Everything in this lecture is one operation, multiplication, repeated once per step,

14
00:00:36,730 --> 00:00:41,027
and you can do the whole thing on the calculator already open in another tab.

15
00:00:41,577 --> 00:00:46,299
Read the ninety-five percent column first, because it's the one that catches people.

16
00:00:46,299 --> 00:00:48,942
Ninety-five percent per step is genuinely good.

17
00:00:48,942 --> 00:00:51,753
Individually, you'd approve it without hesitation.

18
00:00:51,753 --> 00:00:56,475
Chained across ten steps, the whole task now finishes correctly sixty percent of the

19
00:00:56,475 --> 00:00:56,756
time.

20
00:00:56,756 --> 00:00:58,330
That's not a rounding error.

21
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:00,917
That's a coin flip wearing a good report card.

22
00:01:00,917 --> 00:01:02,997
So what's actually driving that drop?

23
00:01:02,997 --> 00:01:04,121
Just multiplication.

24
00:01:04,121 --> 00:01:08,450
If each step succeeds independently with probability p, and nothing catches a

25
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:13,173
mistake before the next step builds on it, the whole chain succeeds with probability

26
00:01:13,173 --> 00:01:15,253
p multiplied by itself once per step.

27
00:01:15,253 --> 00:01:19,301
Five steps at ninety-five percent isn't ninety-five percent minus a bit.

28
00:01:19,301 --> 00:01:23,911
It's zero point nine five to the fifth power, seventy-seven percent, because every

29
00:01:23,911 --> 00:01:26,947
single step has to land for the task to count as done.

30
00:01:27,497 --> 00:01:30,907
Walk the five steps out loud and it stops feeling abstract.

31
00:01:30,907 --> 00:01:35,646
Point nine five, times point nine five, is point nine oh two five after two steps,

32
00:01:35,646 --> 00:01:37,554
already under ninety-one percent.

33
00:01:37,554 --> 00:01:41,368
Times point nine five again is point eight five seven after three.

34
00:01:41,368 --> 00:01:43,738
Again is point eight one five after four.

35
00:01:43,738 --> 00:01:47,380
Again is point seven seven four after five, matching the table.

36
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:50,559
Each individual multiplication barely moves the number.

37
00:01:50,559 --> 00:01:53,102
Five of them in a row move it twenty points.

38
00:01:53,102 --> 00:01:57,899
Now picture that fifth step being the one that actually issues the refund, or sends

39
00:01:57,899 --> 00:01:59,402
the email a customer sees.

40
00:01:59,402 --> 00:02:02,812
The four steps before it were invisible to anyone watching.

41
00:02:02,812 --> 00:02:04,141
The compounding wasn't.

42
00:02:04,691 --> 00:02:09,170
Look at the ninety-nine percent column too, because it's the argument for why that

43
00:02:09,170 --> 00:02:12,337
number matters so much more than it sounds like it should.

44
00:02:12,337 --> 00:02:15,778
The jump from ninety-five to ninety-nine per step looks modest.

45
00:02:15,778 --> 00:02:20,038
Chained ten deep, it's the difference between a coin flip and a system you can

46
00:02:20,038 --> 00:02:22,386
actually trust with something that matters.

47
00:02:22,386 --> 00:02:26,974
Compare the two columns at ten steps directly: sixty percent against ninety percent.

48
00:02:26,974 --> 00:02:31,506
Four points of per-step accuracy, the gap between ninety-five and ninety-nine, buys

49
00:02:31,506 --> 00:02:35,930
you thirty points of whole-chain reliability once it's compounded ten times over.

50
00:02:35,930 --> 00:02:40,408
That's the entire argument for why teams spend so much effort chasing the last few

51
00:02:40,408 --> 00:02:43,631
points of model accuracy on anything that runs long chains.

52
00:02:43,631 --> 00:02:44,887
It isn't perfectionism.

53
00:02:44,887 --> 00:02:49,037
It's the only lever in that column that actually moves the number this much.

54
00:02:49,587 --> 00:02:51,274
Both responses are legitimate.

55
00:02:51,274 --> 00:02:54,705
They are not equally efficient, and the table is exactly why.

56
00:02:54,705 --> 00:02:58,867
Pushing a step's accuracy from ninety-five to ninety-seven percent is real

57
00:02:58,867 --> 00:03:00,779
engineering work for a small gain.

58
00:03:00,779 --> 00:03:05,447
Cutting a ten-step chain to five steps, or inserting one checkpoint that catches an

59
00:03:05,447 --> 00:03:10,171
error before it compounds into the next three steps, moves the whole-chain number by

60
00:03:10,171 --> 00:03:13,882
tens of points, for a design decision rather than a model upgrade.

61
00:03:13,882 --> 00:03:18,494
When you're scoping an agent, this is the lever worth reaching for first, and it's

62
00:03:18,494 --> 00:03:23,162
the same instinct behind the checkpoint idea Section three's degradation paths were

63
00:03:23,162 --> 00:03:23,893
built around.

64
00:03:23,893 --> 00:03:27,492
A checkpoint doesn't have to mean a person reading every output.

65
00:03:27,492 --> 00:03:32,103
Often it's a cheap rule: confirm the order number the agent pulled actually exists

66
00:03:32,103 --> 00:03:34,859
before letting it decide anything about a refund.

67
00:03:34,859 --> 00:03:37,727
That single check doesn't just catch its own error.

68
00:03:37,727 --> 00:03:42,170
It resets the compounding, because every step after it now starts from verified

69
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:46,557
ground instead of carrying forward whatever the chain assumed five steps back.

70
00:03:47,107 --> 00:03:51,781
Say the honest caveat plainly, because the maths above assumes something that isn't

71
00:03:51,781 --> 00:03:52,513
exactly true.

72
00:03:52,513 --> 00:03:54,541
Real steps aren't fully independent.

73
00:03:54,541 --> 00:03:59,271
Sometimes that helps: a checkpoint catches an error before it compounds, so the real

74
00:03:59,271 --> 00:04:02,143
chain does better than raw multiplication predicts.

75
00:04:02,143 --> 00:04:06,536
Sometimes it hurts: one bad piece of context early in the chain, a wrong order

76
00:04:06,536 --> 00:04:11,098
history, a misread date, quietly drags every step downstream with it, so the real

77
00:04:11,098 --> 00:04:12,055
chain does worse.

78
00:04:12,055 --> 00:04:14,927
Neither correction changes the shape of the lesson.

79
00:04:14,927 --> 00:04:19,602
More autonomous steps between checks means more compounding, in whichever direction

80
00:04:19,602 --> 00:04:21,686
your specific chain happens to break.

81
00:04:21,686 --> 00:04:25,571
Don't let that honesty become an excuse to wave the whole table away.

82
00:04:25,571 --> 00:04:30,133
Nobody in the room disputing this number can tell you, on the spot, whether their

83
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:33,625
agent's steps happen to correlate for the better or the worse.

84
00:04:33,625 --> 00:04:38,130
Treating the multiplied estimate as a reasonable starting point, and checking it

85
00:04:38,130 --> 00:04:42,636
against real production data once you have some, is a far stronger position than

86
00:04:42,636 --> 00:04:45,057
assuming independence doesn't apply to you.

87
00:04:45,607 --> 00:04:50,043
That's the definition worth carrying past today, and the exercise takes less time

88
00:04:50,043 --> 00:04:52,342
than most people expect to be worth doing.

89
00:04:52,342 --> 00:04:53,164
Count honestly.

90
00:04:53,164 --> 00:04:57,708
Every tool call, every branch where the model decides something, not just the steps

91
00:04:57,708 --> 00:04:59,953
that show up on the architecture diagram.

92
00:04:59,953 --> 00:05:01,432
Then do the multiplication.

93
00:05:01,432 --> 00:05:05,539
Most people who run this for the first time on their own proposed agent are

94
00:05:05,539 --> 00:05:10,083
surprised by how far the real number sits below the number they'd have guessed from

95
00:05:10,083 --> 00:05:10,576
the demo.

96
00:05:10,576 --> 00:05:14,792
Bring the result into the next conversation about that agent, whatever it is.

97
00:05:14,792 --> 00:05:17,859
A budget request, a launch review, a debate about scope.

98
00:05:17,859 --> 00:05:22,458
A specific number, computed in front of the room, changes that conversation in a way

99
00:05:22,458 --> 00:05:25,196
that a general worry about reliability never does.

100
00:05:25,196 --> 00:05:29,686
It's the same shift Section four spent a whole section teaching: a measured number

101
00:05:29,686 --> 00:05:33,409
beats a confident feeling every time someone tries to argue with it.

102
00:05:33,959 --> 00:05:35,561
One thing before you move on.

103
00:05:35,561 --> 00:05:39,318
Run the actual computation, on a real chain, not a hypothetical one.

104
00:05:39,318 --> 00:05:43,904
Write the number down even if it's uncomfortable, especially if it's uncomfortable.

105
00:05:43,904 --> 00:05:48,323
Then find one point in that chain where a checkpoint, a human glance or a simple

106
00:05:48,323 --> 00:05:52,135
rule, would split it into two shorter chains instead of one long one.
